the eviction, and never as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-448-that cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-449-exclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-450-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-451-### Where the asynchrony goes\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-452-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-453-The IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-454-whether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:455:a callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-456-not pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:457:boundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-458-arrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-459-every reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-460-when it chooses.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-461-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-462-This is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-463-that the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-464-retained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-465-code to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-479-  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-480-  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-481-  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\ndocs/de