ream.md-581-is also `:dao.stream/ok`.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-582-\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-594-handle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-595-stream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-596-later. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-597-nature.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-598-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-599-`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-600-what *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-601-and `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:602:closes, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-603-`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-604-handle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-605-handle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-606-minted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-607-not to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-608-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-609-Closing an attachment freezes what that handle can still observe: values\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-610-appended to the logical stream afterwards are not observable through it, so\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-611-`end` is reached at the position the stream had reached when the attachment\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-612-closed. And a logical stream that has itself been closed remains attachable —\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-613-`attach!` answers `ok` and the new handle reads retained history to `end` —\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-614-because close changes future availability, not identity or reachability.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-615-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-616-Closing the logical stream changes future availability only:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-617-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:618:- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-619-- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:620:  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-621-- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-622-  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-623-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-624-There is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-625-authoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-626-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-627-## Concurrency\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-628-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-629-Each logical stream is one append-only sequence, and a handle's writer\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-630-surface is on exactly one sequence — for some transports the logical stream\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-631-itself, for others the ordered outbound path toward a stream elsewhere (see\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-632-Writing). A successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-633-in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on, and every operation's\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:634:outcome reflects that sequence at one moment. DaoStream guarantees nothing\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-635-further about concurrent operations.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-636-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-637-This follows from Axiom 2. The sequence a handle's surfaces are on is the one\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-638-truth for that handle; each outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-639-reader that observes\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:640:`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-641-contradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-642-view exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-643-answered by the sequence the handle's writer surface is on — the value has a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-644-position in it or it does not — and where that sequence is not observable from\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-645-this handle, the answer is read where it is.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-646-What an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-647-a merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-648-and interpretation happens above the stream.\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-650-## Retention and Gaps\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-651-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-652-Retention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-653-stream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-654-them through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-655-the oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-656-buffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-657-`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:658:permanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-659-outcomes and standardizes no policy.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-660-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-661-**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-662-policy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-663-positions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-664-eviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-665-absence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-666-reader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-710-Capacity is the wrong knob for this — a larger window makes loss less likely\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-711-and never makes it reported — so sizing never substitutes for the declaration.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-712-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-713-The obligation a complete-history transport takes on is one sentence: **a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-714-complete-history reader begins at its logical sequence's origin and never\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-715-evicts an element of that sequence.** Every element present at creation, and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-716-every value an append places on that same sequence with `ok`, remains part of\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-717-it for as long as the logical stream exists. If the medium cannot serve\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:718:retained history it reports `transport-error`; it never silently substitutes a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-719-suffix. Everything else follows. Its `:oldest` anchor stays at the origin, so\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-720-`gap` is impossible and is excluded for that reason.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-721-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-722-What it owes on the writing side depends on what it is. A complete-history\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-723-transport need not be writable at all — a finite immutable history is a valid\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-724-one, and owes no writer outcome. A writable one that declares a finite capacity\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-725-returns `full` on reaching it, without appending and without evicting: refusing\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-726-and evicting are the two answers to the same condition, and a transport that has\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-727-given up one owes the other. A writable one with no declared capacity — a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-728-logically unbounded log, however it grows or spills beneath — may exclude\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-729-`full`. Logically unbounded is not physically infinite, and the line runs\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-730-between failures an operation can observe and return from and those it cannot:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-731-unexpected exhaustion the transport can observe and return from is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:732:`transport-error`, while fatal host or runtime exhaustion, from which the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-733-operation produces no result at all, lies outside the outcome algebra\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-734-altogether. Neither is ever converted into the eviction of acknowledged\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-735-history. A transport whose every operation either completes its state\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-736-transition or does not return therefore has no firing condition for\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:737:`transport-error` and excludes it like any other outcome.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-738-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:739:## Composition\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-740-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:741:DaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-742-that transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-743-own cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-744-outside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-745-pipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-746-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-747-A forwarder between streams is a single step (not a callback loop) over the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-748-transport-agnostic stream algebra. Because the stream never blocks, cadence is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-749-owned by the composition-supplied driver that repeatedly calls the step,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-750-yielding execution as needed by the host runtime.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-751-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-752-Flow control (a reader pausing a sender) is an interpreter's concern. A pause\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-753-has to be a lease and not a switch to avoid permanently stuck states. Those\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-754-semantics live in `dao.lease.md`, whose facts are datoms on a medium\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:755:(`dao.space`). DaoStream provides the honest baseline: a cursor that falls\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-756-behind is evicted past and told so with a `gap`, and the reader holding it\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-757-decides what that means.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-758-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-759-## Explicitly Absent\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-760-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-761-Absent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-762-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-763-- **an ambient registry** — a namespace-global dispatch table, or registration\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-808-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-809-**The assumption.** The Result Convention makes each operation's outcome set\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-810-exhaustive and makes result *maps* open. The two rules are asymmetric: a future\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-811-extension may add a key, never an outcome. The contract assumes the seven\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-812-outcome sets are complete for every transport that will ever exist.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-813-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-814-**Evidence.** Consumers are already total over the closed sets and treat\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-815-anything else as a fault: `dao.stream.observe/step` classifies an unrecognized\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:816:read or effect outcome as `transport-error` and reports `:defect` or `:failed`;\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-817-`yin.vm.engine/handle-put` and `handle-next` throw. *Envelopes* declines to\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-818-reserve an outcome for gating and says gating \"arrives as an addition\", but\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-819-under the exhaustive rule a new outcome is not an addition.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-820-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-821-**What breaks.** Any condition the present outcomes cannot express honestly:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-822-attachment or append refused for lack of authority; a descriptor that is valid\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-823-but served elsewhere now; a transport that is throttling rather than full; an\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-824-append whose effect is unknown (OD-2). Each must either be squeezed into an\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-832-> A consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-833-> `ok`; nothing observed and nothing appended; and not retryable, unless the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-834-> result map carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-835-> unrecognized outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-836-> assembly.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-837-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-838-With that rule a newer transport degrades to \"refused, do not retry\" on an\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-839-older consumer instead of to an exception, and `:dao.stream/retry?` lets it\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:840:say when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-841-their meanings and need not carry the key.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-842-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-843-**Cost if deferred.** Every consumer written in the meantime hard-codes\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-844-\"unknown means defect\", and the first new outcome is a coordinated change\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-845-across all of them.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-846-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:847:### OD-2. `append!` assumes its own effect is always knowable\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-848-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:849:**The assumption.** *Writing* defines `transport-error` as \"the transport\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-850-failed to perform the append; nothing was appended\", and `ok` as acceptance at\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-851-a definite position. Both assume the transport knows, at return time, whether\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-852-the value is in the sequence.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-853-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-854-**Evidence.** True of all three transports today: `ringbuffer` and `memory-log`\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-855-are single in-memory state transitions, and `ws` sidesteps the question because\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-856-its writer surface is the outbound path, so `ok` claims only enqueueing. No\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-857-transport yet has a medium that can fail *after* accepting bytes.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-858-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-859-**What breaks.** A durable file whose write succeeds and whose sync fails; a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-860-log replicated to a quorum; an append forwarded to a remote sequencer. Each has\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-861-a third state, *unknown*, that the table cannot express. A consumer written\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:862:against the present text retries on `transport-error`, which is correct only if\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-863-nothing was appended, and duplicates the value when something was.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-864-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:865:Two related gaps compound it. `append!`'s `ok` carries no correlation value, so\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-866-where the real answer is displaced to another channel (*Writing*: \"what becomes\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-867-of the value at the far end is reported there, not here\") nothing in the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-868-contract ties that answer to the append that caused it. `attach!` has\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:869:`:dao.stream/attachment` for exactly this; `append!` has no counterpart. And\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-870-`ok` promises neither readability nor delivery nor durability, with no named\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-871-way to learn any of the three later.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-872-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-873-**Proposed resolution.** Three sentences, no new operation:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-874-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:875:1. Reword `transport-error` on `append!`: \"The transport failed to perform the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-876-   append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-877-   declares that its failures are clean.\" The three existing transports declare\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-878-   clean failure, so nothing changes for them.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-879-2. State the rule the RPC layer already follows: **deduplication and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-880-   correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must retry safely puts an\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-881-   identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice deduplicates on it.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-882-   The stream never promises exactly-once.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-883-3. Name where later answers land: a transport whose acceptance is not its\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-947-**The assumption.** *Complete history* obliges a transport to retain every\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-948-element \"for as long as the logical stream exists\", and *Close* says close\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-949-\"erases nothing\". Nothing says how a logical stream stops existing. The\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-950-contract assumes storage is either bounded by eviction or kept forever.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-951-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-952-**Evidence.** There is no destroy, truncate, compact, or redact anywhere in the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-953-surface or in any transport. `memory-log` ends only when its process does. What\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-954-`next`, `cursor`, or `attach!` answer for a stream whose medium was removed out\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:955:of band is undefined; the nearest outcomes are `transport-error` and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-956-`not-found`, and the document chooses neither.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-957-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-958-**What breaks.** Reclaiming disk under a durable log; snapshot-and-truncate\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-959-compaction, the ordinary way a replayed log stays bounded; erasure that law or\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-960-a user requires. All three are forbidden to a transport that declared complete\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-961-history, and consumers are wired to that declaration as a correctness\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-962-requirement.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-963-\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-968-   cursor into the dropped prefix receives `gap` exactly as under eviction, and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-969-   the result map may carry, under a transport-owned key, a descriptor or\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-970-   content address for the summary that replaces the prefix. A consumer that\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-971-   requires complete history is not wired to one; a consumer that can resume\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-972-   from a summary is. No new outcome is needed.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-973-2. State the end of existence: **destruction is transport-owned**, like any\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-974-   operation outside the seven. After it, `attach!` answers `not-found`; a live\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-975-   handle's `next` and `cursor` answer `end` if the handle was closed first and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:976:   `transport-error` otherwise; `descriptor` still answers `ok`, because a name\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-977-   outlives what it named. Redaction of a single element is not a stream\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-978-   operation at all: under *taking is a write* it is a retraction datom, and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-979-   what must be unreadable is made so by discarding a key held elsewhere.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-980-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-981-**Cost if deferred.** The first durable transport ships as \"complete\", gains\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-982-consumers that depend on that word, and can then never reclaim space without\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-983-breaking them.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-984-\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1032-answers a question (a lookup, a query against a remote peer, a lease grant, a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1033-name resolution) faces the same three shapes. Without a rule each will choose\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1034-by local convenience, and the convenient choice on the JVM is the one that\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1035-does not port.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1036-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1037-**Proposed resolution.** One rule, stated here because this is where the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1038-argument for it lives, and one consequence, stated in `dao.jing.md`:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1039-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:1040:1. Add to *Composition*:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1041-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1042-   > The IO model is inherited. An interpreter's portable interface is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1043-   > expressed the way this contract's is: a step that returns what is true\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1044-   > now, including \"not yet\", over streams the caller supplied. A waiting\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1045-   > call or a callback may wrap that step as **host policy**, named as such\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1046-   > and confined to the hosts that can afford it, exactly as\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1047-   > `connect-content!` wraps the stepped client. It is never the interface\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1048-   > another interpreter is written against.\n--\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1069-index.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1070-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1071-### Recorded, not proposed\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1072-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1073-These came out of the same review. Each is reachable by addition under the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1074-rules the contract already has, so none needs a decision now. They are listed\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1075-so that a later plan does not rediscover them as surprises.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1076-\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:1077:- **A writer does not learn where its value landed.** `append!`'s `ok` carries\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1078-  no cursor. Read-your-writes and causal references between streams arrive as\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1079-  an optional `:dao.stream/cursor` key on `ok`. Conditional append (\"only if\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1080-  the tail is still here\") would be an eighth operation; the design does not\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1081-  need it because it assumes **one writer per logical stream**, which\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1082-  `dao.space` practices and this document should say once in *Concurrency*.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1083-- **History is not verifiable.** Identity is an opaque value nothing\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1084-  authenticates, and append-only is the transport's promise, not something a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md-1085-  reader can check. Self-certifying identities and signed or hash-chained\n--\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc-1-(ns dao.stream.ringbuffer\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:2:  \"Fixed-capacity, evict-oldest DaoStream v2 reference transport.\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc-3-  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc-4-\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc-5-\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc-6-(def transport-type :dao.stream/ringbuffer)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc-7-(def capacity-ke